        'A project take-over is often the start of a consulting assignment: the previous lead has gone, is ill, or the project is stuck. You do not start with a blank page but with someone else’s promises.\n\nThe first two days are about understanding, not changing. Whoever overturns everything at once finds out later that part of the old agreements was perfectly sound.',
        'Ask five questions. Who decides, and have they said so? What was promised to whom? What is the plan of record: the plan that sponsor and client agreed to? What is late, and why? What does the team think is really wrong?\n\nAsk the last one one-to-one. People rarely say what they think in a group.',
        'Read the documents in a fixed order: project mandate, plan, the last three status reports, the decision list and the risk list. The mandate shows what was intended, the reports what happened.\n\nThe difference between the two is your job. If every report says “green” while actions pile up, the report measures something other than reality.',
        'Then do a status check: a first measurement of where the project really stands, in numbers. How many actions are open, how many are older than four weeks, how many tasks have no owner?\n\nOld open actions are often a sign that nobody is picking them up. The status check is not a verdict on your predecessor but a starting point you can compare with later.',
