        'Many clients already have a route: a PMO (project management office), a client gate model with fixed decision points, mandatory templates and rules. Your route must fit into that, otherwise you are not read and not paid.\n\nThe question is not “which route is best” but “what does this organisation require, and what can I do well within it?”',
        'The PMO often guards the tolerances too: how far a project may deviate in time or money before it must go back to the decider. Sometimes they are tighter than what you would choose, as the lesson Baseline, tolerances and rolling wave planning explains.\n\nDo not only ask what the tolerances are, but who sets them and what happens if you exceed them.',
        'Five questions for the PMO before you propose your own route. Which gates are there and who decides at each? Which templates are mandatory and which are really read? What are the tolerances? Where is my project reported? And: what happens if I shorten a template?\n\nThe answer to the last question says a lot about how strict the rules are in practice.',
